WEBVTT

00:00:00.015 --> 00:00:02.676
Aesop’s Fables. Part 1.

00:00:02.676 --> 00:00:04.651
The Fox and the Grapes.

00:00:04.651 --> 00:00:15.747
A hungry Fox saw some fine bunches of Grapes hanging from a vine that was trained along a high trellis, and did his best to reach them by jumping as high as he could into the air.

00:00:15.747 --> 00:00:30.516
But it was all in vain, for they were just out of reach: so he gave up trying, and walked away with an air of dignity and unconcern, remarking, "I thought those Grapes were ripe, but I see now they are quite sour."

00:00:30.516 --> 00:00:32.954
The Goose that Laid the Golden Eggs.

00:00:32.954 --> 00:00:39.106
A Man and his Wife had the good fortune to possess a Goose which laid a Golden Egg every day.

00:00:39.106 --> 00:00:53.283
Lucky though they were, they soon began to think they were not getting rich fast enough, and, imagining the bird must be made of gold inside, they decided to kill it in order to secure the whole store of precious metal at once.

00:00:53.283 --> 00:00:57.846
But when they cut it open they found it was just like any other goose.

00:00:57.846 --> 00:01:06.015
Thus, they neither got rich all at once, as they had hoped, nor enjoyed any longer the daily addition to their wealth.

00:01:06.015 --> 00:01:08.394
Much wants more and loses all.

00:01:08.394 --> 00:01:10.183
The Cat and the Mice.

00:01:10.183 --> 00:01:13.383
There was once a house that was overrun with Mice.

00:01:13.383 --> 00:01:24.262
A Cat heard of this, and said to herself, "That's the place for me," and off she went and took up her quarters in the house, and caught the Mice one by one and ate them.

00:01:24.262 --> 00:01:30.485
At last the Mice could stand it no longer, and they determined to take to their holes and stay there.

00:01:30.485 --> 00:01:37.522
"That's awkward," said the Cat to herself: "the only thing to do is to coax them out by a trick."

00:01:37.522 --> 00:01:46.485
So she considered a while, and then climbed up the wall and let herself hang down by her hind legs from a peg, and pretended to be dead.

00:01:46.485 --> 00:01:50.706
By and by a Mouse peeped out and saw the Cat hanging there.

00:01:50.706 --> 00:02:03.665
"Aha!" it cried, "you're very clever, madam, no doubt: but you may turn yourself into a bag of meal hanging there, if you like, yet you won't catch us coming anywhere near you."

00:02:03.665 --> 00:02:10.071
If you are wise you won't be deceived by the innocent airs of those whom you have once found to be dangerous.

00:02:10.071 --> 00:02:11.720
The Mischievous Dog.

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:21.493
There was once a Dog who used to snap at people and bite them without any provocation, and who was a great nuisance to every one who came to his master's house.

00:02:21.493 --> 00:02:26.818
So his master fastened a bell round his neck to warn people of his presence.

00:02:26.818 --> 00:02:33.198
The Dog was very proud of the bell, and strutted about tinkling it with immense satisfaction.

00:02:33.198 --> 00:02:39.638
But an old dog came up to him and said, "The fewer airs you give yourself the better, my friend.

00:02:39.638 --> 00:02:44.728
You don't think, do you, that your bell was given you as a reward of merit?

00:02:44.728 --> 00:02:48.080
On the contrary, it is a badge of disgrace."

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:51.058
Notoriety is often mistaken for fame.

00:02:51.058 --> 00:02:53.378
The Charcoal-Burner and the Fuller.

00:02:53.378 --> 00:02:57.801
There was once a Charcoal-burner who lived and worked by himself.

00:02:57.801 --> 00:03:19.836
A Fuller, however, happened to come and settle in the same neighbourhood; and the Charcoal-burner, having made his acquaintance and finding he was an agreeable sort of fellow, asked him if he would come and share his house: "We shall get to know one another better that way," he said, "and, beside, our household expenses will be diminished."

00:03:19.836 --> 00:03:30.783
The Fuller thanked him, but replied, "I couldn't think of it, sir: why, everything I take such pains to whiten would be blackened in no time by your charcoal."
